title: Key invariants
tags: Documentation
kind: overview
audience: dev
aliases: invariants invariant
summary: What must stay true across every change — in the data, the forests, the log, file tracking, the daemon and the GUI — with the note that owns each rule and what checks it.

An invariant here is a rule the rest of the code relies on without checking it
again. Each line states one, names the note that owns it, and says what guards
it when something does. The static guards are the rules of
`.semgrep/invariants.yml`, run by `scripts/check.sh`: they catch what neither the
compiler nor a test of today's code can see — a future write placed in the wrong
layer.

A change that breaks one of these is not a bug fix or a refactoring: it is a
change of design, to be decided as such, with its note rewritten.

!! Data

* A metarecord never holds the same `(name, value)` twice: writes collapse duplicates ([[No duplicate rows]]).
* Setting a field replaces ''every'' row of that name on the metarecord; adding appends ([[Fields and values]], [[The verb pattern]]).
* A field name has one value type in the whole repository, `nothing` aside ([[One value type per field name]]).
* A metarecord's `version` is a hash of its content, maintained by the daemon alone, never set by a client and never restored — restoring the rows restores it. Compare two versions for equality only ([[Metarecord version]]).
* A metarecord belongs to the repository whose store holds it; there is no owner field ([[Metarecord]], [[Repository]]).

!! Forests

* The `tree_ref` values of one field name form a forest: no cycle, at most 1000 deep; a write that would break it is refused with `400` ([[TreeRef forest]]).
* A metarecord holds at most one position per forest ([[One position per forest]]; `crates/daemon/tests/one_position.rs`).
* A forest is referentially closed: a parent must have a position of its own, and a position with children cannot be taken away. The watcher and log navigation are exempt ([[Referential integrity of a forest]]).

!! Writes and the log

* Every write goes through `log::Writer`: one revision is one transaction covering the rows, the log, HEAD and the derived key spaces, so HEAD and the data never disagree ([[Writes and atomicity]], [[Revisions and operations]]; semgrep `mf-write-outside-writer`).
* A write on a loaded repository goes through the repository's own writer, so that its configured retention applies ([[Automatic retention]]; semgrep `mf-writer-bypasses-retention`).
* Log navigation restores what the snapshots recorded — rows with their original ids — and is exempt from the rules a manual write must meet ([[Log storage]]).

!! File tracking

* Tracking is opt-in: nothing is followed until `mf_watch = true` is set ([[Watch and ignore fields]], [[Eligibility]]).
* A file's metarecord outlives the file: on deletion `mfr_path` becomes `nothing`, the metarecord stays ([[Event semantics]]).
* Only the watcher, the orphan commands and a manual `force` write set `mfr_path` to `nothing`; reconcile never does, it leaves the stale path ([[Reconcile]], [[Orphans]]; semgrep `mf-mfr-path-nothing-outside-watcher`).
* The watcher and a reconcile must reach the same state from the same filesystem history ([[Testing the watcher]]).
* Whether a path is there is asked of the entry itself, never through a symlink: a broken link is an ordinary tracked file ([[Symbolic links]]; semgrep `mf-path-exists-follows-symlinks`).
* `.metafolder/internal/` is the only part of a repository excluded from tracking ([[Repository layout on disk]]).

!! The daemon and its stores

* A loaded repository's store is held under an exclusive lock for as long as the daemon holds it: no second daemon on the same repository ([[The repository lock]]).
* The watcher and the executor hold a `Weak<RepoState>`, never an `Arc`, or an unloaded repository would stay alive, locked ([[Why background tasks hold a weak reference]]; semgrep `mf-repostate-arc-in-background-task`).
* Opening a store that is up to date rewrites nothing: the store records the versions of its layout and of its derived data, and a migration runs only when one differs ([[Store tables]]).
* The query engine alone serves a filter, and nothing falls back: a shape it cannot answer is a daemon bug, reported as one ([[No operand runs in SQL]]). The engine and the naive oracle must agree on every query ([[The query oracle]]).
* The cost of a bounded operation does not grow with the repository; it is asserted by counting the keys read, never by timing ([[Reading the log costs the window]]; `crates/daemon/tests/kv_cost.rs`, `log_cost.rs`).
* The sync state of a pair of repositories lives outside the data model, in a per-pair database under `internal/`: it is never in the event log, and changes only while both repositories are loaded in the same daemon (`docs/spec-sync.org`).

!! The wire and the configuration

* Every request to the daemon carries its session token; no route is exempt, `/health` included. The GUI server gates its scripting API and every route serving a file ([[Session tokens]]).
* `core::API_VERSION` is the wire-protocol version, not the crate version: a change to the HTTP contract that would make an older client misbehave bumps it by one; a purely additive change does not ([[Health and diagnostics]], [[Connection to the daemon]]).
* Nothing reaches a remote host ([[No network access]]).
* No program falls back to a built-in default: a missing required file, or a malformed one, stops it ([[No runtime fallback]], [[Shipped defaults in the source tree]]).

!! The GUI

* Every media decoder runs sandboxed, and the GUI refuses to start without the sandbox ([[Sandboxing the media decoders]]; semgrep `mf-unsandboxed-decoder`).
* A file's bytes are never loaded into the WebView as a document ([[Why /fsraw never serves a document]]).
* A panel queries its shadow root, never `document`, which is the shell ([[Writing a panel type]]; semgrep `mf-panel-queries-document`).
* The GUI's JavaScript is typechecked without exception (semgrep `mf-typecheck-suppressed-in-gui-js`).
* The Rust side owns the state of the window; the shell mirrors it ([[GUI]]).

